  <body>
    <main>
      <p class="top" id="en"><a href="/">← KERN</a> · <a href="#it" lang="it">Italiano</a></p>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <small>Last updated 9 October 2026</small>
      <h2>What KERN keeps</h2>
      <p>Without an account, your first name, your chosen field, your answers and drafts, your reflections, your co-pilot chat and your settings are saved only in this browser on this device (local storage). We cannot see them, except what you send to the co-pilot (see below).</p>
      <h2>If you create an account</h2>
      <p>An account is optional. If you create one, we keep your email address, your first name and a copy of the same trail, so it syncs across your devices. Our account and database provider, Supabase, stores them in the EU (Frankfurt, Germany). Access rules make sure no other user can read them, and we look at them only when needed to run the service. Your password is never stored in readable form, only as a secure hash. Supabase also sends the emails that confirm your address and reset your password. You stay signed in through a token saved in this browser, not a cookie.</p>
      <h2>What is sent</h2>
      <p>Nothing you write is sent anywhere unless you write to the co-pilot (see below), or you choose to share it (for example with "Send my answers to the KERN team", which opens your own share sheet or copies the text), or you have an account, in which case your trail goes only to your account and the signs you leave are shown, without your name, to other people on the same mission (see below). There are no cookies and no advertising, and usage counts stay off unless you say yes (see below). When you write to the co-pilot, your last messages and your chosen field (and, if you tap Ask KERN.AI on a mission, that mission and the first 400 characters of your draft) are sent to our AI provider, Anthropic, only so it can write the reply. We do not add your name, so avoid typing personal details in the chat. We do not keep these messages on our servers; Anthropic, a US company, may keep them for a limited time under its own terms, and the transfer from the EU relies on the standard contractual clauses in its terms. Our server keeps your IP address in memory for up to a day to limit abuse. If the service cannot be reached, the co-pilot answers with pre-written questions that run on your device. The hosting provider (Netlify) processes technical data such as your IP address to deliver the page, as every website host does.</p>
      <h2>Sending an answer to a company</h2>
      <p><b>Status:</b> not active yet. Brand missions are samples for now, and no answer goes to any company.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>How it will work:</b> on a brand mission, you can choose to send that one answer to the company behind it. You decide each time, mission by mission. It is off by default: nothing is sent unless you send it yourself.</li>
        <li><b>What is sent:</b> the answer you chose to send and, if you gave one, your first name. Never your email, your password, your other answers, your reflections, your Kern card, your co-pilot chat or your drafts.</li>
        <li><b>Who gets it:</b> only the company behind that mission. From then on it is responsible for what it received (an independent controller under the GDPR).</li>
        <li><b>Legal basis:</b> your consent (GDPR Art. 6(1)(a)), given for each answer you send. It is never a condition for using KERN, and saying no changes nothing about your missions or your Kern card.</li>
        <li><b>Changing your mind:</b> you can stop sending at any time. An answer a company already has is its responsibility; you can ask it to delete it, and we will tell it you asked.</li>
        <li><b>Totals only:</b> a company may also see totals about its own mission, for example how many people finished it. Totals are anonymous and cannot be linked back to you.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Tips for the next person</h2>
      <p>After a mission you can leave a short tip for the next person (up to 140 characters; the code calls it a "sign"). Without an account it stays on this device. With an account, it is shown to other people who open the same mission, together with the date, but never with your name, email or any other detail about you. Tips that contain links, email addresses or long numbers are not shared. Deleting that answer removes its tip; deleting your account removes all of them.</p>
      <h2>Co-op with a friend</h2>
      <p>Two missions in every field can be finished with a friend. When you tap "Finish it with a friend" or "Send to a friend", KERN builds a link that carries your first name and your answer (up to 500 characters) after the # sign, and opens your own share sheet or copies the link. The part after # is never sent to our servers, but the app you send the link through can read it, and so can anyone who has the link. Your friend's reply travels back the same way, up to 300 characters with their first name. KERN keeps both texts only on your device (and in your account, if you have one) so you can read them in yourKERN, and removes them with "Delete my data". Nothing is sent unless you choose to send the link.</p>
      <h2>Usage counts, without your name</h2>
      <p><b>Off unless you say yes.</b> KERN asks once on the Home screen, and you can change your mind any time in Settings. If you say yes, your device makes a random code (not linked to your name, your email or your account) and sends one short line to our database at Supabase (EU, Frankfurt) each time something happens: you said yes, you opened KERN that day, opened a mission, sent an answer, saved a reflection, tapped Share, or asked KERN.AI for help. Each line holds the random code, the kind of event, the field and mission number, whether it is a brand mission, your language and the time. The random code and the times are still personal data, because they can single out one device over time. That is why this is your choice.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Never sent:</b> your name, your email, anything you write (answers, drafts, reflections, chat), or your account. Your IP address is not stored in our table; Supabase and Netlify process it technically to deliver the request, as any website host does.</li>
        <li><b>Why:</b> to count how many people use KERN and which missions work, so we can improve it, and to show partners and investors totals, never individuals.</li>
        <li><b>Legal basis:</b> your consent (GDPR Art. 6(1)(a); the random code kept on your device also needs it under the ePrivacy rules). It is never a condition for using KERN.</li>
        <li><b>Who sees it:</b> the KERN team. Everyone else gets totals.</li>
        <li><b>How long:</b> at most 12 months, then we delete it.</li>
        <li><b>Switching off and deleting:</b> in Settings, switch off Usage counts: we stop counting and delete what was counted for this device straight away. Delete my data, Log out and Delete my account do the same. If you are offline at that moment, the earlier lines stay until they are deleted at 12 months. The random code is the only link to your device, so if you lose the device we cannot find your lines for you; they go the same way.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Your control</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Export: Settings, then Export my data, downloads your trail as a file: your answers, reflections, drafts, chat and settings. The usage-count choice and its random code are not in it.</li>
        <li>Delete: Settings, then Delete my data, removes it from this device. Clearing your browser data does the same.</li>
        <li>Account: Log out removes your trail from this device and keeps it in your account. Delete my account removes the account and the synced trail for good.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Age</h2>
      <p>KERN is for people aged 18 and over for now, because it is built for adults and keeps what you write.</p>
      <h2>Not therapy</h2>
      <p>KERN is not therapy or medical advice. If you are struggling, please talk to someone you trust or a local helpline. If you are in danger, call your local emergency number.</p>
      <h2>Who runs KERN</h2>
      <p>KERN is built and run by Manuel Di Paolo (Italy), the data controller. Contact: <a href="mailto:manuel@trykern.it">manuel@trykern.it</a>.</p>
